   ['Thomas Hobbes (Leviathan, 1651) argued that in a state of nature without government life would be "solitary, poor, nasty, brutish, and short", so rational people would agree to give power to an absolute sovereign. John Locke (Two Treatises of Government, 1689) held that people have natural rights to life, liberty and property and create government to protect them; a government that violates them may be resisted. Jean-Jacques Rousseau (The Social Contract, 1762) argued that legitimate law expresses the general will of citizens.',
    'John Rawls (A Theory of Justice, 1971) asked which principles people would choose behind a veil of ignorance, not knowing their own wealth, talents, sex or race. He argued they would choose (1) equal basic liberties for all and (2) the difference principle: social and economic inequalities are acceptable only if they benefit the least advantaged and are attached to positions open to all under fair equality of opportunity. Robert Nozick (1974) replied that a distribution is just if it arose from just acquisitions and voluntary transfers, opposing redistribution.',
    'Isaiah Berlin distinguished negative liberty (freedom from interference) and positive liberty (the capacity to be one\'s own master). Debates about rights, democracy, colonialism and global justice continue; African thinkers such as Nyerere (Ujamaa), Nkrumah and Amílcar Cabral linked political philosophy to liberation and development.'],
